// core/mem — value-lifetime hooks.
//
// `Drop` is the one trait here: an `impl Drop for T` gives T a
// finalizer that the reference-counting runtime runs when a value's
// last reference goes away. It is the value-scoped counterpart to
// `defer`, which is function-scoped — a `defer` fires when the
// enclosing call returns no matter who still holds the value, while a
// `drop` fires exactly when the value itself dies.
//
//     impl mem.Drop for FileWriter {
//         function drop(self: Self) { self.handle.close(); }
//     }
//
// The compiler calls `drop(self)` from the type's generated drop glue,
// at the point the refcount reaches zero and BEFORE the value's fields
// are reclaimed — so the body can still read every field.
//
// Do not call it yourself: an explicit `v.drop()` would run the body a
// SECOND time, on a value the runtime is still going to finalize. The
// checker rejects one (E073).
//
// A `Drop` type does not participate in Perceus reuse. Reuse hands a
// dying value's box straight to the next constructor of the same shape
// instead of freeing it, which would skip the finalizer entirely, so the
// optimisation is declined for these types — the cost of declaring one.
//
// Reliability tracks the RC implementation, not this trait. A value the
// reclamation passes cannot yet prove dead — see the leak classes in
// docs/RC-PERCEUS-SELF-HOST-PORT.md — simply never reaches rc-zero, and
// its `drop` never runs. `Drop` is a cleanup hook, not a guarantee; do
// not hang correctness on it firing.
pub trait Drop {
    function drop(self: Self): void;
}
